{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"logprobs":null,"message":{"content":"{\"plans\":[{\"description\":\"p0 \u2014 Minimal-period row extrapolation. For each row, infer the shortest repeating period from the observed leftmost four cells, then continue that period across all 11 columns. Constant rows remain constant; rows such as [1,7,1,7] and [7,2,7,2] continue as alternating sequences. This exactly produces the observed output. Distinguishing assumption: the shortest valid period is preferred, so a four-cell prefix may really have period 1 or 2 rather than period 4.\",\"id\":\"p0\"},{\"description\":\"p1 \u2014 Four-column tile repetition. Treat the first four columns of every row as a literal tile and repeat that complete four-column tile horizontally until the row is filled. The observed rows happen to have either constant or two-cell-alternating four-column tiles, so this gives the observed output. Distinguishing assumption: the tile width is always four, even when a shorter period exists; a nonperiodic four-cell prefix would be repeated as a block rather than minimized.\",\"id\":\"p1\"},{\"description\":\"p2 \u2014 Two-column parity rule. Infer each row's even-column and odd-column colors from its first two cells, and fill the entire row by alternating those two colors. The observed input satisfies this rule in every row, including the all-1 rows and the alternating colored rows, so the given output follows. Distinguishing assumption: only the first two columns matter; any differences in columns three and four would be ignored when making a prediction.\",\"id\":\"p2\"},{\"description\":\"p3 \u2014 Reflected-prefix continuation. Regard the first four cells of each row as a short edge pattern and extend it by alternating the prefix with its reversal, producing a reflection-like continuation. For the observed prefixes, which are all constant or alternating, this is indistinguishable from the shown continuation and therefore fits the output. Distinguishin